Certificate-Induced Knowledge Geometry:

A Riemannian Framework for Certified Scientific Discovery

Ouadi Maakoul

AI : Grok XAi, chatGpt, Claude, Meta AI, Deepseek 

Abstract

We develop a rigorous Riemannian geometric framework for the structure of certified scientific knowledge. The central object is the certified knowledge manifold \mathcal{K}, whose points are model–policy–certificate triples and whose metric is pulled back from a natural Riemannian metric on the certificate space \mathcal{C} via the map that extracts the mathematical guarantees. Every structural property is analysed under explicitly stated assumptions, and a clear distinction is maintained between proved statements, conjectures, and open problems.

The framework is illustrated in full detail for the linear quadratic stabilisation of a double integrator, with the space of positive‑definite matrices as Lyapunov certificate space. We provide closed‑form expressions for the pullback metric, the knowledge potential, and its Hessian. A corrected computation shows that the intrinsic discovery curvature vanishes identically, while the Euclidean discovery curvature diverges as the reciprocal square of the distance to the stability boundary. This sharpens the central thesis: the curvature that governs discovery is an artefact of the parameterisation, not a feature of the certified knowledge itself.

Scientific discovery is modelled as a first‑order gradient flow on \mathcal{K}, and convergence results for Hadamard manifolds are recalled. The work concludes with a discussion of generic certificate‑wall behaviour and an outlook on modelling paradigm shifts via stratified certificate atlases, laying rigorous foundations for future theoretical and algorithmic development.

