Chapter 2

Mathematical Preliminaries

We fix notation and recall essential concepts. Standard references are do Carmo [18] for Riemannian geometry, Bhatia [7] for positive definite matrices, and Bačák [6] for Hadamard spaces.

2.1 Riemannian manifolds and pullback metrics

A smooth manifold M equipped with a metric tensor g is Riemannian. The Levi‑Civita connection \nabla is the unique torsion‑free connection compatible with g. Geodesics satisfy \nabla_{\dot\gamma}\dot\gamma = 0. A Hadamard manifold is a simply connected, complete Riemannian manifold of non‑positive sectional curvature.

If f: M \to N is a smooth immersion and (N, h) is a Riemannian manifold, the pullback metric f^*h is defined by

(f^*h)_p(u,v) = h_{f(p)}(df_p u, df_p v), \qquad u,v \in T_p M.

If f is only an immersion, the pullback is still a well‑defined Riemannian metric. The geometry of M so obtained reflects the geometry of the image and the way it is embedded, and it is independent of any coordinate choice on M in the sense that a diffeomorphism of M that leaves the map f unchanged leaves the metric invariant.

2.2 The manifold of positive‑definite matrices

Let \mathcal{P}_n be the set of n \times n real symmetric positive‑definite matrices, an open convex cone in \operatorname{Sym}(n). It becomes a Hadamard manifold when equipped with the affine‑invariant metric

g_P(X,Y) = \operatorname{tr}(P^{-1} X P^{-1} Y), \qquad X,Y \in T_P\mathcal{P}_n \cong \operatorname{Sym}(n).

The geodesic joining P_0 to P_1 is given by

P(t) = P_0^{1/2}\bigl(P_0^{-1/2} P_1 P_0^{-1/2}\bigr)^t P_0^{1/2},

and the Riemannian distance is

d_{\mathcal{P}}(P_0,P_1) = \bigl\| \log(P_0^{-1/2} P_1 P_0^{-1/2}) \bigr\|_F.

The function U(P) = \log\det P is geodesically affine with respect to this metric: along a geodesic P(t) = P_0^{1/2} e^{tS} P_0^{1/2} with S = P_0^{-1/2} X P_0^{-1/2} (where X \in T_{P_0}\mathcal{P}_n),

\log\det P(t) = \log\det P_0 + t \operatorname{tr}(S),

so its second derivative along any geodesic vanishes identically.

Alternative metrics on \mathcal{P}_n exist, most notably the Log‑Cholesky metric [30], which is bi‑invariant, computationally efficient, and free from the swelling effect sometimes observed with the affine‑invariant metric. The choice of metric on the certificate space is part of the modelling process and will alter the induced geometry on \mathcal{K}.

2.3 Lyapunov theory and verification

Consider a linear system \dot x = A_{\text{cl}} x. The matrix A_{\text{cl}} is Hurwitz if and only if there exists P \succ 0 solving the Lyapunov equation

P A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top P = -Q

for some Q \succ 0. In this case V(x) = x^\top P x is a quadratic Lyapunov function certifying exponential stability. The map from A_{\text{cl}} to P is a rational diffeomorphism for a fixed Q.

Modern certification encompasses far richer classes: sum‑of‑squares (SOS) Lyapunov functions for polynomial systems [19], neural Lyapunov functions verified by CEGIS loops [33], control barrier functions, and contraction metrics. A comprehensive survey is given in [17]. The abstract certificate space \mathcal{C} may thus contain matrices, polynomials, neural network weights, and scalar robustness margins, all equipped with appropriate Riemannian metrics.

