Chapter 5

Illustrative Example: Linear Quadratic Stabilisation of a Double Integrator

We work out the complete geometry for the double integrator \ddot y = u with state x = (y, \dot y)^\top. The policy is linear state feedback u = -K x with K = (k_1, k_2) \in \mathbb{R}^{1\times 2}. The closed‑loop matrix is

A_{\text{cl}} = A - BK,\qquad
A = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix},\quad
B = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.

The set of stabilising gains is \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} = \{ K \mid k_1 > 0,\; k_2 > 0\}, which we identify with the manifold of models (the open first quadrant).

Fix the performance matrix Q = I_2. For every K \in \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}}, the Lyapunov equation

P A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top P = -I \tag{5.1}

has a unique solution P \succ 0. The certificate space is \mathcal{C} = \mathcal{P}_2 with the affine‑invariant metric. The certificate‑extraction map is

\Phi: \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} \to \mathcal{P}_2, \qquad \Phi(K) = P(K).

5.1 Explicit solution and immersion property

Solving (5.1) for P = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{pmatrix} gives

\begin{aligned}
b &= \frac{1}{2k_1}, \\
c &= \frac{1 + k_1}{2k_1 k_2}, \\
a &= \frac{k_1^2 + k_1 + k_2^2}{2k_1 k_2}.
\end{aligned} \tag{5.2}

The determinant is

\det P = \frac{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2}{4k_1 k_2^2}. \tag{5.3}

Lemma 5.1 (Immersion and embedding).
The map \Phi is injective and an immersion; hence it is a smooth embedding. The pullback metric g^{\mathcal{K}} = \Phi^* g^{\mathcal{P}_2} makes \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} a Riemannian manifold.

Proof. From (5.2), K can be uniquely recovered from P:

k_1 = \frac{1}{2b}, \qquad k_2 = \frac{c - b}{2b c}.

Thus \Phi is injective. To verify the immersion property, let V \in T_K \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} and differentiate (5.1):

dP\, A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top dP = P B V + V^\top B^\top P. \tag{5.4}

If dP = 0, the right‑hand side vanishes. Since P \succ 0 and B is a column vector, the only way the rank‑1 matrix P B V + V^\top B^\top P is zero is V = 0. Hence d\Phi_K is injective. ∎

5.2 Pullback metric and knowledge potential

The pullback metric is

g^{\mathcal{K}}_K(V,W) = \operatorname{tr}\!\bigl(P^{-1} dP_V\, P^{-1} dP_W\bigr),

where dP_V solves (5.4). The knowledge potential, with U(P) = \log\det P, becomes

\mathcal{U}(K) = \log\det P(K) = \log\frac{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2}{4k_1 k_2^2}. \tag{5.5}

5.3 Intrinsic discovery curvature

Proposition 5.2 (Flat intrinsic geometry).
On \mathcal{P}_2 with the affine‑invariant metric, the function U(P) = \log\det P is geodesically affine. Since \Phi is an injective immersion, the pullback Hessian satisfies \operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{K}}} \mathcal{U} \equiv 0, and consequently

\kappa_{\text{int}}(K) \equiv 0 \qquad \forall K \in \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}}.

The certified knowledge manifold for this problem is intrinsically flat with respect to the discovery curvature.

This is a sharp realisation of the thesis: the certificate‑induced geometry sees no “curvature” in the space of stabilising gains; the optimisation landscape is completely flat when viewed intrinsically.

5.4 Physical curvature and blow‑up at the stability boundary

We now examine the Hessian of \mathcal{U} with respect to the standard Euclidean metric on \mathbb{R}^2. From (5.5),

\mathcal{U}(k_1,k_2) = \log\bigl((k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2\bigr) - \log(4k_1) - 2\log k_2.

The gradient and Hessian are readily computed. The most singular behaviour occurs as k_2 \to 0^+ (with k_1 fixed positive). Differentiating,

\frac{\partial \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2} = \frac{2k_2}{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2} - \frac{2}{k_2},

\frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2^2} = \frac{2(k_1+1)^2 - 2k_2^2}{\bigl((k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2\bigr)^2} + \frac{2}{k_2^2}.

As k_2 \to 0^+, the first term approaches 2/(k_1+1)^2, but the second term diverges as 2/k_2^2. Therefore,

\frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2^2} \sim \frac{2}{k_2^2} \to \infty.

The mixed derivatives remain bounded, and \partial^2 \mathcal{U}/\partial k_1^2 behaves similarly near k_1 \to 0^+. The Frobenius norm of the physical Hessian is dominated by this singularity:

\| \operatorname{Hess}_{\text{Eucl}} \mathcal{U} \|_F \sim \frac{2}{k_2^2} \to \infty.

Proposition 5.3 (Physical curvature blow‑up).
As K approaches the stability boundary \partial \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} (i.e., k_2 \to 0^+ or k_1 \to 0^+), the physical discovery curvature \kappa_{\text{phys}}(K) diverges to infinity.

This blow‑up is a consequence of the unbounded growth of the Lyapunov matrix (and its determinant) required to certify arbitrarily slow exponential decay. It is entirely an artefact of the Euclidean parameter metric; the intrinsic geometry, as shown in Proposition 5.2, is perfectly regular.

