Chapter 6

Scientific Discovery as Gradient Flow

We model a rational research trajectory as a curve \gamma(t) on \mathcal{K} that descends the knowledge potential. The simplest dynamics is the first‑order Riemannian gradient flow

\dot\gamma(t) = -\operatorname{grad}_{\mathcal{K}} \mathcal{U}(\gamma(t)), \qquad \gamma(0) = x_0. \tag{6.1}

This equation is the continuous‑time limit of steepest descent in the Riemannian metric. More complex second‑order models involving inertia are possible, but here we concentrate on the overdamped limit for clarity.

6.1 Convergence on Hadamard manifolds

Proposition 6.1 (Convergence of gradient flow).
Suppose \mathcal{K} is a Hadamard manifold and \mathcal{U}: \mathcal{K} \to \mathbb{R} is geodesically convex. Then the gradient flow (6.1) converges to the unique global minimiser of \mathcal{U}.

Proof. This is a standard result for gradient flows on Hadamard spaces; see, e.g., Bačák [6, Theorem 1.2]. Non‑positive curvature and convexity prevent cycles and guarantee monotonic descent. ∎

Remark 6.2 (Applicability to the double integrator).
In the example of Chapter 5, \mathcal{K} is an open subset of \mathbb{R}^2 with the pullback metric from \mathcal{P}_2. While \mathcal{P}_2 is Hadamard, a submanifold with the induced metric need not inherit non‑positive sectional curvature (the Gauss equation introduces a positive‑definite second fundamental form term). Whether \mathcal{K} is Hadamard is an open question even for this simple case. The flatness of the potential (Proposition 5.2) makes the gradient flow trivial: \operatorname{grad}_{\mathcal{K}} \mathcal{U} \equiv 0, so every point is stationary. For general certificates, the Hadamard property and geodesic convexity must be investigated on a case‑by‑case basis.

Even without global convexity, local convergence can often be guaranteed via the Łojasiewicz inequality or by restricting to basins of attraction.

