Chapter 8

Paradigm Shifts via Stratified Certificate Atlases

The most forward‑looking extension of the framework is to view \mathcal{K} not as a single manifold but as a union of manifolds \mathcal{K}_\alpha corresponding to distinct certification principles (quadratic Lyapunov, barrier functions, sum‑of‑squares, neural certificates, etc.). Each stratum carries its own certificate space and extraction map \Phi_\alpha. The full space of certified models becomes a Whitney stratified space; Riemannian metrics on such spaces have been developed in the context of musical chords [13, 14] and provide a ready‑made geometric toolkit.

8.1 Whitney stratified spaces

A Whitney stratified space is a topological space X decomposed into a disjoint union of smooth manifolds (strata) such that the closure of each stratum is a union of strata, and Whitney’s regularity conditions hold. A Riemannian metric on X can be defined by specifying compatible metrics on each stratum, together with rules for measuring distances between points in different strata.

8.2 Application to certified knowledge

For certified knowledge, the strata are naturally indexed by the certificate types \alpha. A discovery flow that reaches a point where \Phi_\alpha is no longer an immersion can continue in a new stratum \mathcal{K}_\beta whose certificate map extends the guarantees. The transition rules are governed by the compatibility of the certificates in the ambient space \mathcal{C}. This picture offers a mathematically precise formulation of a “paradigm shift” and connects naturally with the practical reality that different certification methods apply to different regions of the model space.

Example 8.1. A controller may initially be certified by a quadratic Lyapunov function. As performance demands push the system towards the stability boundary, the quadratic certificate becomes singular (the Lyapunov matrix grows without bound). At the boundary, one can switch to a higher‑degree SOS Lyapunov function, entering a new stratum with a larger certificate space and a different pullback metric. The local geometry near the stratum boundary and the behaviour of geodesics crossing strata are fertile grounds for future investigation.

