Chapter 9

Conclusion and Outlook

This dissertation has presented a rigorous Riemannian geometric framework for certified scientific knowledge. The certified knowledge manifold \mathcal{K}, equipped with the pullback metric from the certificate space \mathcal{C} and a utility‑derived potential, captures the notion that models should be compared through their guarantees, not their parameterisations. The double‑integrator example demonstrated the full machinery analytically, yielding a striking contrast: the intrinsic geometry is flat, while the physical curvature diverges at the stability boundary. The physical blow‑up is an artefact of the parameter metric, not a genuine feature of the knowledge landscape.

9.1 Classification of results

· Proved statements: Lemma 3.3 (invariance under reparameterisation); Lemma 5.1 (immersion and embedding for the double integrator); Proposition 5.2 (vanishing intrinsic curvature); Proposition 5.3 (physical curvature blow‑up); Proposition 6.1 (convergence on Hadamard manifolds, citing Bačák).
· Conjectures: Conjecture 7.1 (generic blow‑up at certificate walls).
· Definitions and framework: Certified knowledge manifold, pullback metric, knowledge potential, discovery curvature (intrinsic and physical), gradient flow dynamics.
· Open problems:
  1. Sufficient conditions for \mathcal{K}_0 to be a smooth manifold for broad certificate classes.
  2. Conditions under which \Phi is an immersion; methods to compute the pullback metric in practice.
  3. Characterisation of the sectional curvature of \mathcal{K}; determining when it is Hadamard.
  4. Geodesic convexity of \mathcal{U} for non‑trivial certificates.
  5. Rigorous proof of Conjecture 7.1 using stratified Morse theory.
  6. Detailed geometry of stratified certificate atlases, including geodesics crossing strata and transition rules.
  7. Algorithms for computing geodesics on \mathcal{K} and simulating the gradient flow, bridging the gap with metric learning and Riemannian optimisation.

The present work lays a rigorous single‑stratum foundation upon which these stratified extensions and algorithmic developments can be built. It is hoped that the geometric perspective will illuminate the structure of certified knowledge and inspire new approaches to automated scientific discovery.


Appendix A

Mathematical Background

This appendix collects the essential definitions and theorems from Riemannian geometry and related areas that underpin the dissertation. Standard references are do Carmo (1992), Lee (2018), and Bačák (2014).

A.1 Riemannian manifolds

A Riemannian manifold (M,g) is a smooth manifold M equipped with a smoothly varying inner product g_p on each tangent space T_pM. The tensor g is called the metric tensor.

For a smooth curve \gamma:[a,b]\to M, its length is

L(\gamma) = \int_a^b \sqrt{g_{\gamma(t)}(\dot\gamma(t),\dot\gamma(t))}\,dt .

The Levi‑Civita connection \nabla is the unique affine connection on M that is torsion‑free and compatible with g; i.e., for all vector fields X,Y,Z,

\nabla_X Y - \nabla_Y X = [X,Y], \qquad
X\,g(Y,Z) = g(\nabla_X Y, Z) + g(Y,\nabla_X Z).

A curve \gamma is a geodesic if its velocity field is parallel along itself: \nabla_{\dot\gamma}\dot\gamma = 0.

The Riemann curvature tensor is

R(X,Y)Z = \nabla_X \nabla_Y Z - \nabla_Y \nabla_X Z - \nabla_{[X,Y]} Z .

The sectional curvature of a plane spanned by orthonormal vectors u,v is K(u,v) = g(R(u,v)v,u).

A manifold is complete if every geodesic can be extended indefinitely. A Hadamard manifold is a simply connected, complete Riemannian manifold of non‑positive sectional curvature (K\leq 0). Hadamard manifolds are diffeomorphic to \mathbb{R}^n via the exponential map at any point.

A.2 Pullback metrics

Let (N,h) be a Riemannian manifold and f:M\to N a smooth map. If f is an immersion (i.e., its differential df_p:T_pM\to T_{f(p)}N is injective for all p), then the pullback metric f^*h on M is defined by

(f^*h)_p(u,v) = h_{f(p)}(df_p u, df_p v),\qquad u,v\in T_pM .

The pair (M, f^*h) is then a Riemannian manifold. The map f is an isometric immersion if, in addition, f preserves lengths of curves. Here we use the pullback to induce a metric on M, so the geometry of M is entirely determined by f and the metric on N.

If f is only an immersion and not injective, the pullback metric remains a well‑defined Riemannian metric; distinct points in M with the same image are at positive distance if the map is locally injective but globally they may be identified in a quotient.

A.3 Geodesic convexity and gradient flows

A function F:M\to\mathbb{R} on a Riemannian manifold is geodesically convex if for every geodesic \gamma:[0,1]\to M the composition F\circ\gamma is convex in the usual sense:

F(\gamma(t)) \leq (1-t)F(\gamma(0)) + tF(\gamma(1)),\quad \forall t\in[0,1].

The Riemannian gradient of F is the unique vector field \operatorname{grad} F satisfying g(\operatorname{grad}F, X) = dF(X) for all vector fields X. In local coordinates, (\operatorname{grad}F)^i = g^{ij}\partial_j F.

The Riemannian Hessian is the covariant derivative of the gradient:

\operatorname{Hess}F (X,Y) = g(\nabla_X \operatorname{grad}F, Y) .

The gradient flow of F is the dynamical system

\dot\gamma(t) = -\operatorname{grad}F(\gamma(t)) .

If M is a Hadamard manifold and F is geodesically convex and bounded below, the gradient flow converges to a global minimiser (Bačák, 2014). The Łojasiewicz inequality often provides local convergence even without global convexity.

A.4 Stratified spaces

A Whitney stratified space is a topological space X decomposed into a disjoint union of smooth manifolds (strata) such that the closure of each stratum is a union of strata, and Whitney’s regularity conditions (A and B) hold. A Riemannian metric on a stratified space can be given by assigning a smooth metric to each stratum and ensuring compatibility across strata; this allows defining lengths of curves that cross strata. The space of musical chords (Tymoczko, 2011; Burlet et al., 2021) is a concrete example of a stratified Riemannian manifold. In this dissertation, the stratified viewpoint is used to model paradigm shifts as transitions between different certificate atlases.

