Appendix B

Geometry of Symmetric Positive‑Definite Matrices

We recall the Riemannian structure of \mathcal{P}_n, the set of n\times n real symmetric positive‑definite matrices.

B.1 The affine‑invariant metric

\mathcal{P}_n is an open convex cone in the vector space \operatorname{Sym}(n). For P\in\mathcal{P}_n, the tangent space is T_P\mathcal{P}_n \cong \operatorname{Sym}(n). The affine‑invariant metric (Pennec, 2006; Bhatia, 2007) is

g_P(X,Y) = \operatorname{tr}(P^{-1} X P^{-1} Y),\qquad X,Y\in\operatorname{Sym}(n).

It is invariant under the action of the general linear group: for any invertible A\in GL(n), the map P\mapsto A P A^\top is an isometry.

The geodesic joining P_0,P_1\in\mathcal{P}_n is

P(t) = P_0^{1/2}\bigl(P_0^{-1/2} P_1 P_0^{-1/2}\bigr)^t P_0^{1/2}, \qquad t\in[0,1].

The Riemannian distance is

d_{\mathcal{P}}(P_0,P_1) = \bigl\| \log(P_0^{-1/2} P_1 P_0^{-1/2}) \bigr\|_F,

where \|\cdot\|_F is the Frobenius norm.

Proposition B.1. (\mathcal{P}_n, g) is a Hadamard manifold (simply connected, complete, non‑positive sectional curvature).

Proof. See Bhatia (2007, Chapter 6). ∎

B.2 Special functions

Consider the function U(P)=\log\det P. Its differential is

dU_P(X) = \operatorname{tr}(P^{-1}X).

For any geodesic P(t)=P_0^{1/2}e^{tS}P_0^{1/2} with S = P_0^{-1/2}X P_0^{-1/2},

\log\det P(t) = \log\det P_0 + \log\det e^{tS} = \log\det P_0 + t \operatorname{tr}(S).

Thus U is geodesically affine; its Riemannian Hessian vanishes identically.

B.3 Alternative metrics

The Log‑Cholesky metric (Lin, 2019) uses the Cholesky decomposition P = LL^\top with L lower triangular with positive diagonal. It turns the space into a Lie group with a bi‑invariant metric, yielding simple geodesics and avoiding the swelling effect sometimes observed with the affine‑invariant metric. Both metrics induce the same topology, but the geometry of the pullback on \mathcal{K} will differ depending on which metric is chosen on \mathcal{C}.

