Appendix C

Lyapunov Theory and Certificate Spaces

C.1 Lyapunov equation

For a Hurwitz matrix A_{\text{cl}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n} and a given Q\succ 0, the continuous‑time Lyapunov equation

P A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top P = -Q

has a unique solution P\in\mathcal{P}_n. The solution map A_{\text{cl}}\mapsto P is a real‑analytic diffeomorphism onto its image when restricted to the set of Hurwitz matrices (for fixed Q). The Lyapunov function V(x)=x^\top P x certifies global exponential stability of \dot x = A_{\text{cl}}x.

C.2 Certificate extraction map

In general, a certificate type is a Riemannian manifold (\mathcal{C}^{(k)}, g^{(k)}). The full certificate space is the product \mathcal{C} = \prod_k \mathcal{C}^{(k)} with the weighted product metric. The certificate extraction map is \Phi: \mathcal{K}_0 \to \mathcal{C}, \Phi(m,\pi,c) = c. When a model–policy pair can admit several distinct certificates, one must select a canonical one or include the certificate as part of the point. The present work assumes that a specific certificate is attached to each triple, so \Phi is simply the projection.

