Appendix D

Detailed Proofs for the Double Integrator Example

This appendix contains the complete analytic derivations for the double integrator example of Chapter 5.

D.1 Solving the Lyapunov equation

The closed‑loop matrix is

A_{\text{cl}} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} k_1 & k_2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ -k_1 & -k_2 \end{pmatrix}.

With Q = I_2, the Lyapunov equation P A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top P = -I becomes

\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ -k_1 & -k_2 \end{pmatrix}
+
\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -k_1 \\ 1 & -k_2 \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{pmatrix}
= -\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.

This yields the system

\begin{aligned}
-2b k_1 &= -1 \quad \Rightarrow \quad b = \frac{1}{2k_1},\\
a - b k_2 - c k_1 &= 0,\\
2b - 2c k_2 &= -1 .
\end{aligned}

From the third equation, 2b - 2c k_2 = -1 \Rightarrow c k_2 = b + \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{2k_1} + \frac{1}{2}. Hence

c = \frac{1 + k_1}{2k_1 k_2}.

From the second equation, a = b k_2 + c k_1 = \frac{k_2}{2k_1} + \frac{1+k_1}{2k_2} = \frac{k_2^2 + k_1(1+k_1)}{2k_1 k_2}. Thus

a = \frac{k_1^2 + k_1 + k_2^2}{2k_1 k_2}.

The determinant is computed as

\det P = a c - b^2 = \frac{k_1^2 + k_1 + k_2^2}{2k_1 k_2}\cdot\frac{1+k_1}{2k_1 k_2} - \frac{1}{4k_1^2}.

A direct algebraic simplification yields

\det P = \frac{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2}{4k_1 k_2^2}. \tag{D.1}

D.2 Proof of Lemma 5.1 (Immersion and embedding)

Injectivity. From the formulas above, k_1 = 1/(2b) and k_2 = (c-b)/(2bc). Since P\succ 0 guarantees b>0, c>0, these expressions define a smooth inverse, proving injectivity.

Immersion. Let V = (v_1, v_2)\in T_K\mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} and denote dP = d\Phi_K(V). Differentiating the Lyapunov equation P A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top P = -I gives

dP\,A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top dP = P B V + V^\top B^\top P. \tag{D.2}

The right‑hand side is

R = P \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} v_1 & v_2 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} P
= \begin{pmatrix} b v_1 & b v_2 \\ c v_1 & c v_2 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} v_1 b & v_1 c \\ v_2 b & v_2 c \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2b v_1 & b v_2 + v_1 c \\ b v_2 + v_1 c & 2c v_2 \end{pmatrix}.

Suppose dP=0. Then R=0. From the (1,1) entry, 2b v_1=0 \Rightarrow v_1=0 (since b>0). From the (2,2) entry, 2c v_2=0 \Rightarrow v_2=0. Hence V=0, so d\Phi_K is injective. Therefore \Phi is an immersion and, together with injectivity, an embedding. ∎

D.3 Pullback metric tensor

Although an explicit closed form for g^{\mathcal{K}} is not needed for the curvature results, it is instructive to compute its components. The tangent vector V maps to dP via the solution of the Sylvester equation (D.2). This equation can be solved by vectorising: let \mathrm{vec}(dP) and \mathrm{vec}(R) be column vectors. Then

(I \otimes A_{\text{cl}}^\top + A_{\text{cl}}^\top \otimes I)\,\mathrm{vec}(dP) = \mathrm{vec}(R).

The pullback metric is then

g^{\mathcal{K}}_K(V,W) = \mathrm{vec}(dP_V)^\top (P^{-1}\otimes P^{-1})\,\mathrm{vec}(dP_W).

This is a rational function of K with no singularities in the interior.

D.4 Proof of Proposition 5.2 (Vanishing intrinsic curvature)

The knowledge potential is \mathcal{U}(K) = U(\Phi(K)) with U(P)=\log\det P. On \mathcal{P}_2 with the affine‑invariant metric, the Riemannian Hessian of U satisfies \operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{P}}}U = 0 because U is geodesically affine (Appendix B.2). Since \Phi is an isometric immersion (with respect to the pullback metric), the Hessian of the pullback function is the pullback of the Hessian. More precisely, for any vector fields X,Y on \mathcal{K},

\operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{K}}}\mathcal{U}(X,Y) = \operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{P}}}U(d\Phi(X), d\Phi(Y)) \circ \Phi .

Because the right‑hand side vanishes identically, \operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{K}}}\mathcal{U}=0. Hence \kappa_{\text{int}} = \|\operatorname{Hess}\mathcal{U}\|_{g^{\mathcal{K}}} = 0. ∎

D.5 Proof of Proposition 5.3 (Physical curvature blow‑up)

With Euclidean parameter metric g^{\text{param}} = dk_1^2 + dk_2^2, the physical potential \mathcal{U}(k_1,k_2) = \log\det P is given by (D.1). Write

\mathcal{U} = \log\bigl((k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2\bigr) - \log(4k_1) - 2\log k_2 .

Partial derivatives:

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_1} &= \frac{2(k_1+1)}{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2} - \frac{1}{k_1},\\
\frac{\partial \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2} &= \frac{2k_2}{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2} - \frac{2}{k_2}.
\end{aligned}

Second derivatives:

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_1^2} &= \frac{2[(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2] - 4(k_1+1)^2}{[(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2]^2} + \frac{1}{k_1^2} 
= \frac{2(k_2^2 - (k_1+1)^2)}{[(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2]^2} + \frac{1}{k_1^2},\\
\frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2^2} &= \frac{2[(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2] - 4k_2^2}{[(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2]^2} + \frac{2}{k_2^2}
= \frac{2((k_1+1)^2 - k_2^2)}{[(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2]^2} + \frac{2}{k_2^2},\\
\frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_1 \partial k_2} &= \frac{-4k_2(k_1+1)}{[(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2]^2}.
\end{aligned}

Asymptotics as k_2 \to 0^+ (with k_1>0 fixed):

\frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2^2} \sim \frac{2}{(k_1+1)^2} + \frac{2}{k_2^2} \sim \frac{2}{k_2^2} \to +\infty.

The mixed derivative is O(1/k_2^3)? Let's check: as k_2\to 0, numerator is -4k_2(k_1+1), denominator \sim (k_1+1)^4, so \partial_{12}\mathcal{U} \sim -4k_2(k_1+1)/(k_1+1)^4 = O(k_2), bounded. Thus the Hessian matrix is dominated by the (2,2) entry which blows up like 2/k_2^2. The Frobenius norm satisfies

\|\operatorname{Hess}_{\text{Eucl}}\mathcal{U}\|_F \sim \frac{2}{k_2^2} \to \infty .

The same blow‑up occurs as k_1\to 0^+. Hence \kappa_{\text{phys}} diverges at the stability boundary. ∎

