Appendix F

Certificate Walls and Generic Blow‑Up

F.1 Formal statement of the conjecture

Conjecture F.1 (Generic blow‑up at certificate walls).
Let \mathcal{K} be a smooth manifold with boundary \partial\mathcal{K}, and let \Phi:\mathcal{K}\to\mathcal{C} be a smooth map into a Riemannian manifold \mathcal{C} such that \Phi is an immersion on the interior \mathcal{K}^\circ. Suppose that the certificate condition defining the boundary is given by the vanishing of a scalar function \rho:\mathcal{K}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} with \partial\mathcal{K}=\{\rho=0\}, and that the utility U:\mathcal{C}\to\mathbb{R} has a logarithmic singularity as the certificate approaches degeneracy, i.e., U(\Phi(x)) = \alpha \log\rho(x) + \text{smooth} with \alpha\neq 0. Then, for any smooth Riemannian metric g^{\text{param}} on \mathcal{K}, the physical discovery curvature \kappa_{\text{phys}} diverges as x\to\partial\mathcal{K}.

Supporting evidence. The double integrator example satisfies the hypotheses with \rho(k_1,k_2)=k_2 (or k_1), U=\log\det P, and indeed \kappa_{\text{phys}}\sim 2/k_2^2\to\infty. The blow‑up arises from the second derivative of \log\rho, which contributes -\alpha/\rho^2 times gradient‑type terms.

F.2 Sketch of a possible proof

Under transversality assumptions, one can use a local coordinate system (y^1,\dots,y^{n-1},\rho) near the boundary, where \rho=0 defines the wall. The potential becomes \mathcal{U}(y,\rho) = \alpha\log\rho + f(y,\rho) with f smooth. The Hessian in these coordinates includes terms \partial^2(\alpha\log\rho)/\partial\rho^2 = -\alpha/\rho^2. As \rho\to 0^+, this term dominates the Hessian’s operator norm, provided the coordinate change from the parameter space to (y,\rho) has bounded geometry. A rigorous proof would involve estimating the induced metric on the boundary stratum and applying the Morse lemma with parameters. This is left to future work.

