Appendix G

Additional Remarks on Smoothness and Immersion Conditions

G.1 Smoothness of \mathcal{K}_0

The set \mathcal{K}_0 = \{(m,\pi,c)\mid c\text{ valid for }(m,\pi)\} is typically defined by equalities and strict inequalities. When the certificate condition can be expressed as F(m,\pi,c)=0 with F a smooth map and 0 a regular value, \mathcal{K}_0 is an embedded submanifold. For Lyapunov certificates, the condition P\succ 0 is an open inequality, so the set of strictly positive definite matrices is an open cone, hence a manifold. For neural certificates verified by SMT or CEGIS, the set is semialgebraic and may have corners; a stratified treatment is more faithful.

G.2 Immersion condition

The immersion condition \operatorname{rank}(d\Phi_x) = \dim\mathcal{K} ensures that \Phi locally embeds \mathcal{K} into \mathcal{C}. In the double integrator, we verified this via the rank‑1 argument. In general, if the certificate is uniquely determined by the model–policy pair and the map is smooth with injective differential, the pullback metric is non‑degenerate. When \Phi fails to be an immersion at some points, those points form the certificate wall, and a different certificate type (a new stratum) may be required.

